::CW_U06_L06_Q01::What should an author presentation do before the excerpt is read?{
~A. Avoid giving any context at all.#Incorrect. The audience needs enough context to understand the excerpt.
=B. Introduce the title, genre, brief context, and listening focus.#Correct. These elements prepare the audience to understand the work.
~C. Explain every event in the full story.#Incorrect. Too much summary can take over the presentation.
~D. Apologize for the piece before reading.#Incorrect. The presenter should guide the audience confidently and clearly.
}

::CW_U06_L06_Q02::Which statement best identifies presentation purpose?{
~A. I want to talk until time runs out.#Incorrect. This is not a learning or presentation purpose.
~B. I want to avoid explaining the piece.#Incorrect. The standard requires communication of creative work.
~C. I want the audience to guess everything.#Incorrect. An author presentation should guide understanding.
=D. I want the audience to understand how my revised dialogue shows tension.#Correct. This gives a clear craft-focused purpose.
}

::CW_U06_L06_Q03::A student chooses a long chapter that requires ten minutes of plot explanation. What is the best revision to the plan?{
=A. Select a shorter excerpt that shows the key craft choice with brief context.#Correct. A focused excerpt better supports craft reasoning.
~B. Keep the whole chapter because longer is always stronger.#Incorrect. Long excerpts can weaken focus.
~C. Remove all context and hope the audience follows.#Incorrect. The audience still needs a clear frame.
~D. Replace the presentation with a written quiz.#Incorrect. The task is author presentation planning.
}

::CW_U06_L06_Q04::Which choice is plot summary instead of craft reasoning?{
~A. I used repeated s sounds to slow the poem's ending.#Incorrect. This is craft reasoning because it names sound and effect.
~B. I revised dialogue so the reader can infer discomfort.#Incorrect. This is craft reasoning because it connects revision to effect.
=C. The character leaves home, meets a friend, and returns later.#Correct. This tells events but does not explain writer choices.
~D. I placed the final image alone so the audience pauses.#Incorrect. This explains line break effect.
}

::CW_U06_L06_Q05::Which speaking note style best supports a presentation?{
=A. Short key phrases in the planned sequence.#Correct. Key phrases help the presenter remember ideas while still addressing the audience.
~B. A full essay copied word for word in tiny font.#Incorrect. This can lead to flat reading and lost audience contact.
~C. No notes at all even if the sequence is complex.#Incorrect. Notes help maintain organization.
~D. Random words with no connection to the excerpt.#Incorrect. Notes should support the plan.
}

::CW_U06_L06_Q06::Why should the presenter name a listening focus?{
~A. It replaces the need to read the excerpt.#Incorrect. The focus prepares the excerpt; it does not replace it.
~B. It allows the presenter to skip craft reasoning.#Incorrect. The presenter still needs to explain the choice after the excerpt.
~C. It hides the purpose from the audience.#Incorrect. It clarifies purpose.
=D. It tells the audience what craft choice to notice during the excerpt.#Correct. A listening focus guides attention and supports understanding.
}

::CW_U06_L06_Q07::Which is the strongest craft explanation?{
~A. I wrote a poem because poems are short.#Incorrect. This does not explain a specific craft choice.
=B. I placed the final line by itself so the reader pauses before the speaker's decision.#Correct. This names a line break choice and explains reader effect.
~C. I like the ending most.#Incorrect. Preference is not craft reasoning.
~D. My story has an ending.#Incorrect. This is too general to show craft reasoning.
}

::CW_U06_L06_Q08::Which revision reflection is strongest?{
~A. I fixed stuff.#Incorrect. This is vague and does not identify growth.
~B. I changed nothing and learned nothing.#Incorrect. This does not show process reflection.
=C. I revised the opening image to establish setting before conflict, which makes the scene easier to enter.#Correct. This names a revision and explains its effect.
~D. I made it better somehow.#Incorrect. This is too vague for mastery.
}

::CW_U06_L06_Q09::Which sequence best matches the lesson presentation structure?{
~A. Excerpt, unrelated story, apology, ending.#Incorrect. This lacks clear framing and craft reasoning.
~B. Full plot summary, full draft history, no excerpt.#Incorrect. The audience needs focused presentation of the work.
~C. Title only, then stop.#Incorrect. A presentation needs more than identification.
=D. Title and genre, context, listening focus, excerpt, craft reasoning, revision reflection.#Correct. This sequence prepares, presents, explains, and reflects.
}

::CW_U06_L06_Q10::A presenter says, "This is a poem about rain." What should be added to improve the plan?{
=A. A craft focus, such as image, sound, line break, or tone.#Correct. Topic alone is not enough; the audience needs craft direction.
~B. A longer unrelated biography.#Incorrect. Extra unrelated information does not strengthen the craft focus.
~C. A statement that no one needs context.#Incorrect. The audience benefits from useful framing.
~D. A promise that the poem has no choices.#Incorrect. Creative work is built from choices.
}

::CW_U06_L06_Q11::Which excerpt is easiest for an audience to understand?{
~A. A section that starts mid-conflict with no names, no context, and unclear speakers.#Incorrect. This would require too much guessing.
~B. The entire portfolio read without pause.#Incorrect. That is too broad for a focused presentation.
=C. A focused section introduced with a brief explanation of situation and speaker.#Correct. This gives the audience enough information to follow.
~D. A section the presenter cannot explain.#Incorrect. The presenter must be able to explain craft choices.
}

::CW_U06_L06_Q12::Which statement best communicates author growth?{
~A. I wrote it the night before.#Incorrect. This does not explain creative growth.
=B. My first draft explained the fear directly; my revision used action so readers could infer it.#Correct. This compares draft and revision while explaining reader effect.
~C. I did not revise anything.#Incorrect. This does not show process development.
~D. The assignment was difficult.#Incorrect. Difficulty alone does not describe growth in the work.
}

::CW_U06_L06_Q13::What is the role of audience frame?{
=A. It gives readers or listeners the context they need before the excerpt.#Correct. Audience frame prepares the audience without overexplaining.
~B. It hides the title from the audience.#Incorrect. The title should usually be clear.
~C. It replaces the excerpt.#Incorrect. The frame supports the excerpt.
~D. It lists unrelated facts about the writer.#Incorrect. The frame should connect to the work.
}

::CW_U06_L06_Q14::A student's presentation notes include only "read poem, done." What is missing?{
~A. More silence.#Incorrect. Silence does not replace planning.
~B. Random decoration.#Incorrect. Decoration does not build presentation content.
~C. A hidden file name.#Incorrect. The issue is missing presentation structure.
=D. Title, context, listening focus, craft reasoning, and reflection.#Correct. These elements are needed for a complete author presentation plan.
}

::CW_U06_L06_Q15::Which choice best explains why an excerpt should not require too much setup?{
~A. Context is never allowed.#Incorrect. Brief context is important.
~B. Long setup proves the piece is complex.#Incorrect. Complexity does not excuse unclear presentation.
=C. The audience should spend most of the presentation understanding the work and craft, not sorting through long background.#Correct. Brief context keeps the presentation focused.
~D. The presenter should avoid all explanation.#Incorrect. Craft explanation is required.
}

::CW_U06_L06_Q16::Which closing statement best fits an author presentation?{
~A. That is all, and I do not know why I picked it.#Incorrect. This weakens purpose and reflection.
=B. This revision helped the final scene feel more tense because the reader now infers the conflict through dialogue.#Correct. This closes by connecting revision, craft, and effect.
~C. I hope it was not confusing.#Incorrect. A stronger close explains the work's effect.
~D. The story is over.#Incorrect. This does not reflect on craft or growth.
}

::CW_U06_L06_Q17::Which question should guide excerpt selection?{
~A. Is this excerpt the longest possible?#Incorrect. Length does not guarantee quality.
~B. Can I avoid talking about the excerpt?#Incorrect. The presenter must discuss the excerpt.
~C. Will this confuse the audience?#Incorrect. Confusion is not the goal.
=D. Does this excerpt show a craft choice I can explain clearly?#Correct. The excerpt should support craft reasoning.
}

::CW_U06_L06_Q18::Which presentation plan best avoids summary-only speaking?{
=A. Name two craft choices and explain how each affects the reader.#Correct. This moves beyond plot into author reasoning.
~B. List every event in order and stop.#Incorrect. That is summary-only.
~C. Read the entire piece without any introduction.#Incorrect. The audience needs frame and focus.
~D. Say the piece is good without evidence.#Incorrect. Evaluation needs craft evidence.
}

::CW_U06_L06_Q19::Which detail belongs in brief context before an excerpt?{
~A. Every assignment the student completed all semester.#Incorrect. That is too broad and unrelated to the immediate excerpt.
=B. The speaker has just missed the last bus and is deciding whether to call home.#Correct. This gives the situation needed to understand the excerpt.
~C. A list of favorite songs.#Incorrect. This does not frame the creative work.
~D. A full explanation of the ending before reading.#Incorrect. Too much context can reduce the excerpt's impact.
}

::CW_U06_L06_Q20::Which answer best describes communication of craft reasoning?{
~A. Saying the piece is finished.#Incorrect. Completion is not craft reasoning.
~B. Listing page numbers only.#Incorrect. Page numbers do not explain effect.
=C. Explaining how specific writing choices shape meaning or audience effect.#Correct. Craft reasoning connects choice to effect.
~D. Avoiding all terms from the lesson.#Incorrect. Craft vocabulary helps explanation.
}

::CW_U06_L06_Q21::A presenter plans to discuss five unrelated craft choices in two minutes. What should the presenter do?{
=A. Choose two or three focused points that connect to the purpose.#Correct. A focused plan is clearer and more teachable.
~B. Speak faster and keep all five.#Incorrect. Speed can reduce clarity.
~C. Remove the excerpt to make room.#Incorrect. The excerpt is central to the presentation.
~D. Add more unrelated points.#Incorrect. This would increase confusion.
}

::CW_U06_L06_Q22::Which phrase best signals a listening focus?{
~A. Listen to everything and nothing.#Incorrect. This is too vague to guide attention.
~B. I have no focus for this excerpt.#Incorrect. A planned presentation needs focus.
=C. Listen for the repeated hard consonant sounds in the final stanza.#Correct. This tells the audience exactly what craft choice to notice.
~D. Ignore the language choices.#Incorrect. Craft choices are central to the presentation.
}

::CW_U06_L06_Q23::Which statement is too vague for mastery?{
~A. I used dialogue to reveal tension between the characters.#Incorrect. This is a specific craft claim.
~B. I revised sentence length to change pacing.#Incorrect. This names a craft revision.
~C. I selected the excerpt because it shows the narrator's voice.#Incorrect. This explains selection with craft purpose.
=D. I made it better.#Correct. This does not identify what changed or how it affects the audience.
}

::CW_U06_L06_Q24::What should the presenter practice for timing and clarity?{
~A. Only the final word.#Incorrect. The full presentation needs practice.
=B. The sequence of title, context, excerpt, craft explanation, and reflection.#Correct. Practicing the sequence helps the presentation stay organized.
~C. A different assignment.#Incorrect. Practice should match the presentation.
~D. Avoiding the excerpt completely.#Incorrect. The excerpt is part of the presentation.
}

::CW_U06_L06_Q25::Which final plan best meets the lesson standard?{
=A. A plan with title, genre, brief context, selected excerpt, listening focus, craft reasoning, and revision reflection.#Correct. This includes the key author presentation elements.
~B. A plan that only says the work is creative.#Incorrect. This is not enough for presentation mastery.
~C. A plan with no excerpt and no craft explanation.#Incorrect. The standard requires presenting and explaining creative work.
~D. A plan that only summarizes the plot.#Incorrect. Plot summary alone does not communicate craft reasoning.
}